const semCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Semantics: literal meaning', note: 'What words and sentences MEAN independent of context: bachelor = unmarried male; "Cats kill mice" means what it means in any universe. Truth conditions: a sentence\'s meaning is when it would be true.' },
    { math: '2 · Pragmatics: meaning in use', note: '"Can you pass the salt?" — the literal answer is "yes" (and no salt moves). Grice\'s maxims (be relevant, informative, truthful, brief) explain how we mean MORE than we say: flouting a maxim sends a signal.' },
    { math: '3 · Implicature: the implied layer', note: '"Some students passed" implies "not all did" — though "some" doesn\'t strictly exclude "all". Implicatures are cancellable ("some — in fact all — passed") — that cancellability is the diagnostic.' },
    { math: '4 · Speech acts and indirectness', note: 'Requests are disguised as questions everywhere; politeness systems (Brown & Levinson) manage face. Cross-cultural pragmatics: "no" means different things in different cultures — pragmatics is where intercultural miscommunication lives.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/intermediate/semantics-and-pragmatics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Semantics and pragmatics split meaning's labour: <strong>semantics handles the stable,
    context-free meaning; pragmatics handles what speakers manage to mean with it</strong> —
    implicature, politeness, irony, indirect requests. The gap between "what is said" and "what
    is meant" is where most human communication actually happens.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="truth conditions, Grice's maxims, implicature, speech acts" widget="equationStep" config={semCfg} />

  <h2>Why the gap is systematic, not fuzzy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Presupposition:</strong> "Have you stopped X?" presupposes you did X — meaning smuggled in below the main claim. Lawyers and journalists exploit it; logicians formalise it.</li>
    <li><strong>Relevance theory (Sperber & Wilson):</strong> hearers assume utterances are relevant enough to be worth processing — one principle generates all the Gricean effects. The minimalist rival, and still contested.</li>
    <li><strong>Cross-cultural pragmatics:</strong> directness, silence, honourifics — politeness systems differ radically (Japanese honorific grammar encodes social hierarchy morphologically). "Polite" has no universal definition.</li>
    <li><strong>AI's hardest layer:</strong> LLMs handle literal semantics well and pragmatics approximately — implicature, irony and cultural context remain the frontier of natural language understanding.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Grice, "Logic and Conversation" (1975); Sperber & Wilson, Relevance; Levinson, Pragmatics; Brown & Levinson (1987).</p>
</TopicPage>
